  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "Virginia lawmakers are debating House Bill 2724, which would regulate police use of license plate readers by limiting their use to specific investigations, requiring 30-day data destruction, making misuse a misdemeanor, and allowing Virginia State Police to deploy them on state highways. The bill passed the House and heads to the state Senate, while critics like Justice Forward Virginia warn it expands the surveillance state.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "They are not currently regulated in Virginia, so local police departments have been able to use them as they wish and preserve the data as they wish.",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "This bill will limit the law enforcement use of license plate readers to active criminal investigations and matters involving missing persons, wanted persons, human trafficking, stolen vehicles and license plates",
      "paragraph_idx": 6
    },
    {
      "quote": "This is a massive expansion of the surveillance state, and that's what we are really concerned about",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "It requires that data that is stored to be destroyed within 30 days unless it is needed for on-going investigation or prosecution",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    }
  ],
